  "guide": {"focus":"Static friction holds the block until the component of its weight down the slope exceeds the available grip.","predict":{"prompt":"What happens if the block is changed to a lower-grip material?","options":["It starts sliding","It grips more strongly","Its motion is unchanged"]},"variables":[{"control":"object.properties.material","label":"Block material","body":"body-2-3"}],"steps":[{"text":"Run with the original material and check whether the block stays still."},{"text":"Choose a lower-grip material for the block, press R, and run again.","control":"object.properties.material","body":"body-2-3"},{"text":"Compare speed with the previous run.","open":"measure.graph"}],"expect":"Lower static friction makes the block begin sliding; kinetic friction then controls how quickly it accelerates down the slope.","limits":"The ramp angle stays fixed in this activity. Contact is modelled with rigid surfaces and Coulomb friction."},
